export default {
 "vic": {
  "intake": "{\"title\":\"Rescue and intake record: adult female bare-nosed wombat\",\"subtitle\":\"Wildlife Victoria case 26-04417\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Register entry\",\"items\":[\"Case number: 26-04417 (Wildlife Victoria)\",\"Common name: Bare-nosed wombat\",\"Species code: Not recorded\",\"Date in: Not recorded\",\"Sex (M, F or U): F\",\"Age class: Adult\",\"Main injury: Severe mange over both flanks and back; graze on left shoulder\",\"Cause: Hit by car\",\"Where found: The gravel pull-over on the south side of the Bass Highway, just east of the Kilcunda bridge\",\"Status: In care\"]},{\"heading\":\"Rescue details\",\"items\":[\"Wildlife Victoria case number: 26-04417\",\"Hit by a car on the Bass Highway near Kilcunda the night before intake.\",\"A motorist called it in at about 9 pm.\",\"The carer picked her up at 9.40 pm.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Where it was found\",\"text\":\"Bass Highway near Kilcunda. Exact spot: the gravel pull-over on the south side of the highway, just east of the Kilcunda bridge.\"},{\"heading\":\"Animal\",\"items\":[\"Species: Bare-nosed wombat\",\"Sex: Female\",\"Age: Adult\",\"Weight: About 24 kg\",\"Pouch empty\"]},{\"heading\":\"Condition on arrival\",\"items\":[\"Severe mange over both flanks and her back\",\"Crusting on both ears\",\"Graze on the left shoulder\",\"Walking okay, with no obvious fractures\",\"Alert and hissing\"]},{\"heading\":\"First aid and care given\",\"items\":[\"Placed in the quiet pen with a hide box and water.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Vet\",\"text\":\"Booked with the Wildlife Victoria travelling vet for tomorrow morning.\"}],\"due\":[{\"what\":\"Wildlife Victoria travelling vet visit\",\"when\":\"Tomorrow morning\"}],\"check\":[\"What was the rescue date?\",\"What is the shelter case number?\",\"What is the species code?\"]}",
  "log": "{\"title\":\"Oliver (eastern grey kangaroo joey) care log\",\"subtitle\":\"Thursday\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Summary\",\"text\":\"Oliver gained weight, took all four feeds and is spending more time out of the pouch.\"},{\"heading\":\"Weight\",\"text\":\"2.31 kg this morning, up 40 g since Monday.\"},{\"heading\":\"Feeding\",\"items\":[\"Four bottle feeds of Wombaroo 0.7, 120 mL each.\",\"Took all feeds; the last one a bit slowly.\",\"Started picking at kangaroo grass.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Toileting\",\"text\":\"Toileted after every feed with stimulation. Normal pellets.\"},{\"heading\":\"Behaviour and condition\",\"text\":\"Spending more time out of the pouch and hopping around the room.\"},{\"heading\":\"Wounds and treatment\",\"text\":\"Small scrape on his tail from the pen door, cleaned with saline.\"},{\"heading\":\"To watch\",\"items\":[\"Tail scrape from the pen door. Consider a vet check if it doesn't heal well.\"]}],\"due\":[{\"what\":\"Next weigh\",\"when\":\"Monday\"}],\"check\":[\"Is the formula Wombaroo Kangaroo Milk Replacer >0.7?\",\"Has the pen door been made safe?\"]}",
  "carer-carer": "{\"animal\":\"Common ringtail possum joey (female)\",\"species\":\"Common ringtail possum (Pseudocheirus peregrinus)\",\"kind\":\"wildlife\",\"summary\":\"This furred, eyes-open female ringtail joey of about 120 g came into care yesterday after a cat attack in Wonthaggi, and the vet checked her this morning, found no puncture wounds and dispensed an antibiotic. The goal is to finish her antibiotic, grow and wean her onto native browse with other young ringtails, then soft-release her near where she was found.\",\"photo_findings\":[],\"medications\":[{\"name\":\"Antibiotic dispensed by the vet (to prevent infection after the cat attack)\",\"how\":\"As dispensed by the clinic, following the label\",\"how_often\":\"Once a day\",\"how_long\":\"5 days\",\"tip\":\"Give it at the same time each day so no dose is missed.\"}],\"plan\":[{\"when\":\"Day 5\",\"tasks\":[\"Her antibiotic course finishes after the fifth daily dose.\"]},{\"when\":\"As she spends more time out of the pouch (from about 130 days old)\",\"tasks\":[\"Move her to a larger enclosure with climbing branches.\",\"Join her with other young ringtails through an experienced ringtail carer.\"]},{\"when\":\"From about 250 g\",\"tasks\":[\"Add the odd native flower to her browse, keeping it a very small part of her diet.\"]},{\"when\":\"From about 160 days old\",\"tasks\":[\"Reduce her milk gradually following the Wombaroo chart until she is fully on browse, usually at about 310 g.\"]}],\"housing\":[\"Keep her pouch in a small enclosure with a heat pad under half of it so she can move off if too warm; about 30°C was suggested, so set it to the pouch temperature the app shows for her stage.\",\"Keep the enclosure in a dark, quiet room away from pets, household noise and other animals.\",\"Change pouch liners daily and sterilise bottles, teats and syringes before every feed.\",\"Wash your hands before and after handling her, and wear disposable gloves when cleaning droppings.\"],\"feeding\":[\"Feed Wombaroo Possum Milk Replacer >0.8 (the right stage for her age): mix 25 g of powder (3½ scoops) with 80 mL of warm pre-boiled water to make 100 mL and warm it to about 30°C; never give cow's milk, as it causes diarrhoea in possum joeys.\",\"Feed every 4 hours as directed, sharing the daily amount from the feed calculator across the feeds; the Wombaroo guide suggests 2 to 4 feeds a day at her stage, so I'm not certain of the best number, check with your vet if she starts leaving milk.\",\"After each feed, gently stroke around her bottom with a warm, damp cloth until she wees and poos.\",\"Put fresh native browse (3 to 5 local eucalypt species, stems in water) in her enclosure every day, with no fruit, vegetables or flowers while she is under 250 g, as the wrong diet causes bloat; eating some of her own droppings is normal and healthy for ringtails.\",\"Weigh her every day at the same time, before a feed, and offer water in a separate shallow dish, never mixed into her milk.\"],\"activity\":[\"Handle her only for feeds, toileting and weighing, gently and quietly, so she stays wild.\",\"Ringtails are social, so she will be raised with other ringtails through an experienced carer and released together with them.\",\"Give her leafy branches to climb and forage through.\"],\"care\":[\"Record her weight, feeds, droppings and each antibiotic dose daily, and keep the exact rescue location in Wonthaggi with her records for release.\"],\"daily_checks\":[\"Weight before feed\",\"Antibiotic given\",\"Feeds and toileting\",\"Normal wees and poos\",\"Fresh browse eaten\"],\"log_days\":5,\"feed_calc\":{\"applies\":true,\"what\":\"Wombaroo Possum Milk Replacer >0.8\",\"weight_g\":120,\"stage\":\"Furred, eyes open, about 120 g (roughly 130 days, age factor over 0.8)\",\"percent_body_weight_per_day\":14,\"feeds_per_day\":4,\"basis\":\"Wombaroo Milk Booklet common ringtail chart (12 mL/day at 62 g to 18 mL/day at 135 g), about 17 mL/day at 120 g; 2 to 4 feeds a day for >0.8.\",\"sure\":false},\"release\":{\"ready_when\":[\"Fully weaned and eating native browse well\",\"Healthy and needing no more medical care\",\"Climbing confidently, using a nest or drey, and wary of people\",\"About 600 g\"],\"target_weight\":\"About 600 g for a common ringtail possum\",\"how\":[\"Soft-release her with her ringtail group, with a nest box or drey at the site for shelter.\",\"Release at dusk, never during the day.\",\"Avoid extreme heat, cold, storms or heavy rain.\",\"Release within 24 hours of her being ready.\"],\"where\":\"Release her where she was found in Wonthaggi, or if that spot is unsuitable, at the closest suitable place within her home range.\"},\"escalate\":{\"vet\":[\"Any swelling, redness, heat or discharge on her skin, which can be a late sign of a cat bite\",\"Diarrhoea, or fewer or no droppings\",\"Refusing feeds, or weight going down two days in a row\",\"Quiet, dull or not climbing\"],\"urgent\":[\"Cold, floppy or not responding\",\"Breathing difficulty, or milk coming from her nose\",\"Tight, swollen belly (bloat)\",\"Bleeding or a sudden collapse\"]},\"rehydration\":{\"needed\":false,\"weight_g\":null,\"dehydration_pct\":null,\"steps\":[]},\"species_group\":\"ringtail\",\"next_visit\":\"\",\"sources\":[\"vic-rehabilitator-authorisation-conditions\",\"vic-release-requirements\",\"vic-cop-welfare-wildlife-rehabilitation\",\"vic-shelter-foster-carer-authorisation\",\"vic-rehab-guidelines-part-a\",\"wombaroo-milk-replacers-feeding-guide\",\"wires-possums\"]}",
  "tovet": "{\"title\":\"Vet visit summary: Willow, juvenile wombat\",\"subtitle\":\"\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Text to your vet\",\"text\":\"Hi, Bunurong Wildlife Care here about Willow, a juvenile female wombat in care 5 weeks since her mum was hit on the Inverloch road. She's on the mange treatment you prescribed, last dose 10 days ago. Weight down from 6.2 to 6.0 kg this past week, eating fewer pellets but still grazing, loose droppings for 2 days and scratching more again. Is the weight drop a worry, does she need another mange dose, and when can she go outdoors?\"},{\"heading\":\"Animal\",\"items\":[\"Species: wombat\",\"Sex: female\",\"Age or stage: juvenile\",\"Name: Willow\",\"Current weight: 6.0 kg\"]},{\"heading\":\"Reason for visit\",\"text\":\"Weight loss over the past week, reduced pellet intake, loose droppings and increased scratching while on mange treatment. Review of ongoing mange treatment and readiness for the outdoor enclosure.\"},{\"heading\":\"History in care\",\"text\":\"Came into care five weeks ago after her mother was hit by a vehicle on the Inverloch road. Currently on mange treatment prescribed by the vet.\"},{\"heading\":\"Recent observations\",\"items\":[\"Weight: down from 6.2 kg to 6.0 kg over the last week\",\"Feeding: eating fewer pellets, still grazing\",\"Toileting: loose droppings for the past two days\",\"Behaviour: scratching more again\"]},{\"heading\":\"Current medications and treatment\",\"items\":[\"Mange treatment as prescribed by the vet; last dose given ten days ago\"]},{\"heading\":\"Questions for the vet\",\"items\":[\"Is the weight drop from 6.2 kg to 6.0 kg over the past week a concern?\",\"Does Willow need another mange dose, and if so, when?\",\"When can Willow move to the outdoor enclosure?\"]},{\"heading\":\"What the carer needs back\",\"items\":[\"Written dosing instructions if another mange dose is needed\",\"A decision on when Willow can go to the outdoor enclosure\"]}],\"due\":[],\"check\":[\"Which mange treatment and dose was prescribed?\",\"What date was Willow rescued?\",\"What date was the last mange dose?\"]}",
  "funding": "{\"title\":\"Wildlife Ambulance Trailer and In-the-Wild Wombat Mange Treatment\",\"subtitle\":\"Bass Coast Shire Community Grants, Environment Stream\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Project summary\",\"text\":\"Bunurong Wildlife Care is seeking $8,500 to buy a second wildlife ambulance trailer and a portable wombat burrow-flap kit, and to run a volunteer training day. The burrow-flap kit will let us treat wombats with mange in the wild, so we no longer need to bring every affected wombat into care. The project will run from February to December next year.\",\"items\":[]},{\"heading\":\"About us\",\"text\":\"Bunurong Wildlife Care Inc is a registered charity and authorised wildlife shelter (Wildlife Shelter 15147751) based in Inverloch, Victoria. We rescue, rehabilitate and release native wildlife across Bass Coast and South Gippsland. Our team includes 18 trained volunteers.\",\"items\":[]},{\"heading\":\"The need\",\"text\":\"Last year we took in 214 animals. About a third of them were wombats, and 60 of those wombats had mange. At present we bring wombats with mange into care for treatment. This places heavy demand on our rescue transport and care capacity, and it takes the animals out of their home burrows.\",\"items\":[]},{\"heading\":\"What we will do\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Buy a second wildlife ambulance trailer to increase our rescue and transport capacity.\",\"Buy a portable wombat burrow-flap kit and treatment supplies so we can treat mange in wombats in the wild.\",\"Run a volunteer training day so our volunteers can use the new equipment and treat wombats in the field.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Budget\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Wildlife ambulance trailer: $5,200\",\"Burrow-flap kit and treatment supplies: $2,100\",\"Volunteer training day: $1,200\",\"Total requested: $8,500\"]},{\"heading\":\"Timeline\",\"text\":\"The project will run from February to December next year.\",\"items\":[]},{\"heading\":\"Outcomes and how we will measure them\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"We will record the number of wombats treated for mange in the wild.\",\"We will use trail cameras to record how many treated wombats we see recover.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Partners and support\",\"text\":\"Wildlife Victoria and local vets already refer animals to us.\",\"items\":[]},{\"heading\":\"Why us\",\"text\":\"We are an authorised wildlife shelter with 18 trained volunteers and an established intake of injured and sick wildlife. Last year that intake was 214 animals. Wildlife Victoria and local vets already refer animals to us, and our experience with wombats with mange puts us in a strong position to deliver treatment in the wild.\",\"items\":[]}],\"due\":[{\"what\":\"Project start\",\"when\":\"February next year\"},{\"what\":\"Project end\",\"when\":\"December next year\"}],\"check\":[\"What is the grant application deadline?\",\"Which calendar year will the project run?\",\"How many wombats do you expect to treat?\",\"How many volunteers will attend the training day?\",\"Do you already have trail cameras?\"]}",
  "mop": "{\"title\":\"Short-term care plan: juvenile common brushtail possum\",\"subtitle\":\"Overnight care until the rescuer collects it\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Text to send\",\"text\":\"Hi Jenny, thank you for helping the young possum. Keep it in a closed box with small air holes, lined with a towel, in a dark, quiet room away from pets and children. Wrap the hot water bottle (warm, not hot) in a towel and put it at one end, so the possum can move away if it gets too warm. Cover the box with a towel. Handle it as little as possible and wear gloves if you need to. Food and water can wait for the rescuer. A rescuer will collect it tomorrow morning at about 8am. Please call us back if it becomes cold, floppy or you notice an injury.\"},{\"heading\":\"Right now\",\"items\":[\"Use a box with a secure lid and small air holes, lined with a towel. A slightly larger box than a shoe box is best.\",\"Keep it in the laundry, dark and quiet, with the box covered by a towel.\",\"Handle it as little as possible.\",\"Keep pets and children away.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Basic first aid\",\"items\":[\"Fill the hot water bottle with warm tap water, not boiling. Wrap it in a towel.\",\"Put it at one end of the box, so the possum can move closer or away.\",\"Check the bottle every few hours and refill it when it cools.\",\"Do not give food or water unless a vet, wildlife carer or Wildlife Victoria advises otherwise.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Getting it to a vet or shelter\",\"items\":[\"A rescuer will collect it tomorrow morning at about 8am.\",\"Keep the box closed and covered until they arrive.\",\"This care is short-term only, until the rescuer takes over.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Keep yourself safe\",\"items\":[\"Possums can scratch and bite. Wear long sleeves and gloves, and use a thick towel if you must lift it.\",\"Wash your hands after touching it or the box.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Call back straight away if\",\"items\":[\"It feels cold, goes floppy or can't hold its head up.\",\"It has trouble breathing, or you see blood or a wound.\",\"The rescuer can't come.\",\"Call Wildlife Victoria on 03 8400 7300 (24/7).\"]},{\"heading\":\"Where it was found\",\"text\":\"On the ground in her backyard in Wonthaggi, under the big gum near the back fence.\"}],\"due\":[{\"what\":\"Rescuer collects the possum\",\"when\":\"Tomorrow morning, about 8am\"}],\"check\":[\"Street address or GPS for release site?\",\"Does the shoe box have a secure lid?\",\"Rescuer's name or number to give Jenny?\"]}",
  "vetnotes": "{\"animals\":[{\"patient\":{\"name\":\"\",\"species\":\"Wombat\",\"breed\":\"\",\"sex\":\"Female\",\"age\":\"Adult\",\"weight_kg\":\"24.3\",\"microchip\":\"\",\"animal_id\":\"\",\"intake_type\":\"Wildlife rescue (vehicle strike)\"},\"visit\":{\"type\":\"Clinical consult\",\"clinician\":\"\"},\"subjective\":\"Adult female wombat struck by a vehicle on the Bass Highway near Kilcunda last night. A carer presented her this morning.\",\"objective\":{\"vitals\":{\"temp_c\":\"36.1\",\"hr_bpm\":\"82\",\"rr_bpm\":\"22\",\"bcs\":\"\",\"mm_crt\":\"MM pink, CRT <2 s\",\"hydration\":\"\"},\"findings\":[\"BAR, hissing on handling.\",\"Sarcoptic mange, moderate, affecting approximately 30% of the body surface, with crusting over both flanks and both pinnae.\",\"Early fissuring around the left eye.\",\"Abrasion over the left shoulder, 3 x 2 cm, superficial and contaminated with dirt.\",\"No fractures palpable.\",\"Ambulating normally. No neurological deficits.\",\"Pouch empty. Not lactating.\"],\"photo_findings\":[]},\"assessment\":{\"problems\":[\"1. Sarcoptic mange, moderate (approximately 30% body surface), with crusting of both flanks and pinnae and early periocular fissuring of the left eye.\",\"2. Superficial contaminated abrasion over the left shoulder (3 x 2 cm).\"],\"differentials\":[],\"prognosis\":\"Good\"},\"plan\":{\"procedures\":[\"Abrasion over the left shoulder cleaned with dilute chlorhexidine.\"],\"diagnostics\":[],\"treatments\":[{\"drug\":\"Cydectin (moxidectin) pour-on\",\"dose\":\"0.8 mL/kg (19.4 mL stated), per permit\",\"route\":\"Topical\",\"frequency\":\"Once\",\"duration\":\"\",\"when\":\"given\"},{\"drug\":\"meloxicam (Metacam)\",\"dose\":\"0.2 mg/kg\",\"route\":\"SC\",\"frequency\":\"Once\",\"duration\":\"\",\"when\":\"given\"},{\"drug\":\"Cydectin (moxidectin) pour-on\",\"dose\":\"0.8 mL/kg, per permit\",\"route\":\"Topical\",\"frequency\":\"Weekly\",\"duration\":\"15 weeks\",\"when\":\"continue\"}],\"hospital_plan\":[],\"home_care\":[\"House in a quiet pen with a hide box.\",\"Keep handling to a minimum.\",\"Carer to repeat the Cydectin pour-on weekly for 15 weeks.\",\"Weigh weekly.\"],\"follow_up\":[\"Recheck in 1 week.\"]},\"welfare_notes\":\"\",\"check\":[\"Hydration confirmed before meloxicam was given?\",\"'Side deck tin' means Cydectin (moxidectin) pour-on?\",\"Current permit covers carer dosing of Cydectin in care?\"]}]}",
  "carer-vet": "{\"animal\":\"Adult female wombat, 24.3 kg\",\"species\":\"Common (bare-nosed) wombat (Vombatus ursinus)\",\"kind\":\"wildlife\",\"summary\":\"Thank you for caring for this wombat. She is an adult female with moderate mange and a shallow graze on her left shoulder, and she had her first Cydectin (moxidectin) pour-on and a meloxicam (Metacam) pain-relief dose at the clinic today. The goal is to clear the mange and heal her skin so she can go home to Kilcunda as soon as she is ready.\",\"photo_findings\":[],\"medications\":[{\"name\":\"Cydectin (moxidectin) pour-on (to treat mange)\",\"how\":\"Wearing gloves and eye protection, pour 19.4 mL in a line along her spine.\",\"how_often\":\"Once a week, on the same day each week.\",\"how_long\":\"15 weeks in total (the first dose was given today).\",\"tip\":\"Apply it away from drains, ponds and creeks, because Cydectin is toxic to water insects.\"}],\"plan\":[{\"when\":\"In 1 week\",\"tasks\":[\"Give the second Cydectin dose, on the same weekday as today.\",\"Take her to the vet for her recheck.\"]},{\"when\":\"Week 15\",\"tasks\":[\"Give the last Cydectin dose; the course then stops.\"]}],\"housing\":[\"Keep her in a quiet pen with a hide box and straw bedding, well away from dogs and other animals in care, and care for her last each day.\",\"Keep the pen shaded and cool, because wombats do not sweat and overheat easily.\",\"Remove droppings every day and keep the straw free of cat droppings, which can spread disease.\",\"After she leaves, clean the pen and leave it empty for at least three weeks so mange mites die off.\"],\"feeding\":[\"Offer grass hay and fresh native grasses as her main food.\",\"Give clean water in a heavy dish, changed every day.\",\"Your vet says a few carrots are fine as a treat. Some Victorian guidance warns against carrots, because they are used as rabbit bait, so I'm not certain of this; check with your vet.\",\"Never feed cattle or chicken pellets, as some contain medicines that can kill wombats.\"],\"activity\":[\"Handle her as little as possible, quickly and calmly, and do not try to tame her.\",\"Wear gloves and covered clothing whenever you touch her or her bedding, because mange spreads to people as an itchy rash.\",\"Hot-wash and hot-dry her bedding and towels, and wash your hands well afterwards.\"],\"care\":[\"Clean the shoulder graze once a day with saline.\",\"Weigh her once a week on the same scales.\",\"Record each Cydectin dose (date and amount), her weight and how her skin looks, along with her care record.\"],\"daily_checks\":[\"Eating hay and grass\",\"Graze clean, not hot\",\"Skin round eyes intact\",\"Fresh water\",\"Droppings removed\"],\"log_days\":7,\"feed_calc\":{\"applies\":false,\"what\":\"\",\"weight_g\":24300,\"stage\":\"Adult\",\"percent_body_weight_per_day\":null,\"feeds_per_day\":null,\"basis\":\"Adult wombat eating grass and hay, not a measured milk feed\",\"sure\":true},\"release\":{\"ready_when\":[\"Her coat is growing back over the mange areas.\",\"Her skin and the shoulder graze have healed.\",\"She is eating well, bright and alert.\",\"She is wary of people and dogs.\"],\"target_weight\":\"\",\"how\":[\"Release her within 24 hours of being ready, in mild weather with no heatwave or storm.\",\"Release her about an hour after dusk, ideally at an empty burrow near where she was found.\",\"Open the box and let her leave in her own time.\"],\"where\":\"Release her at the Kilcunda pull-over where she was found, or at the closest suitable spot within her home range.\"},\"escalate\":{\"vet\":[\"She stops eating.\",\"The graze becomes hot, swollen or smelly.\",\"The skin around her eye bleeds.\",\"She grinds her teeth, arches her back or paws at her belly, which are signs of pain.\"],\"urgent\":[\"She becomes wobbly, drowsy, drools or keeps lying down after a Cydectin dose.\",\"She shows heat stress: red feet, drooling onto her chest, or lying on her back.\",\"Flies lay eggs on her skin, or her skin develops a rotting smell.\",\"She collapses or stops responding.\"]},\"rehydration\":{\"needed\":false,\"weight_g\":null,\"dehydration_pct\":null,\"steps\":[]},\"species_group\":\"wombat\",\"next_visit\":\"Recheck with the vet in 1 week\",\"sources\":[\"vic-rehab-guidelines-wombats\",\"vic-cop-welfare-wildlife-rehabilitation\",\"vic-release-requirements\",\"vic-mange-management\",\"vic-rehabilitator-authorisation-conditions\",\"vic-shelter-foster-carer-authorisation\",\"vic-rehab-guidelines-part-a\",\"au-sarcoptic-mange-treatment-sheet\"]}",
  "caresheet": "{\"animal\":\"Adult female wombat\",\"species\":\"Common (bare-nosed) wombat\",\"kind\":\"wildlife\",\"summary\":\"Thank you for caring for this adult female wombat, who was hit by a car on the Bass Highway near Kilcunda; she has no broken bones but has moderate mange, early cracking around her left eye and a dirty graze on her left shoulder, which the vet cleaned, and she had her first mange treatment and a pain-relief injection at the clinic. The goal is to clear her mange over 15 weeks of weekly treatment, let the graze heal, and return her to where she was found.\",\"photo_findings\":[],\"medications\":[{\"name\":\"Cydectin (moxidectin) pour-on (for mange)\",\"how\":\"Pour 0.8 mL for every kg of her weight along her back, using her latest weight (19.4 mL at her current 24.3 kg)\",\"how_often\":\"Once a week, on the same day each week\",\"how_long\":\"15 weeks\",\"tip\":\"I'm not certain your permit covers you giving this in care, so check with your vet before your first dose.\"}],\"plan\":[{\"when\":\"Day 7\",\"tasks\":[\"Weigh her and give your first weekly Cydectin dose.\",\"Vet recheck.\"]},{\"when\":\"Week 15\",\"tasks\":[\"Give the last Cydectin dose; treatment then stops.\"]},{\"when\":\"After week 15\",\"tasks\":[\"Check that all her scabs have fallen off and her skin and eye have healed.\"]}],\"housing\":[\"Keep her in a quiet pen with a hide box, away from people, pets and noise, built so she cannot dig her way out.\",\"Keep her apart from all other wildlife, and feed and clean her last.\",\"Wear gloves and long sleeves when cleaning her pen or handling her, and wash your hands afterwards, because people can catch mange.\",\"Once she has gone, clean her pen and leave it empty for at least three weeks.\"],\"feeding\":[\"Give plenty of fresh grass and good hay every day, as close to her wild diet as you can.\",\"Give fresh water in a heavy, shallow dish, changed every day.\",\"Never give cattle or chicken pellets, because they may contain medicines that can kill wombats.\",\"Never give bread, cereal biscuits, muesli, chaff, clover, cabbage or phalaris grass, because they cause bloat or gum infections, or lack the fibre she needs.\"],\"activity\":[\"Handle her as little as possible; she hisses when handled, so keep clear of her head and move slowly and quietly.\",\"Scatter her grass and hay so she can forage, and give her soil to dig in.\",\"Don't tame her or let visitors see her, so she keeps her fear of people.\"],\"care\":[\"Weigh her once a week on treatment day, and use that weight to work out her Cydectin dose.\",\"In warm weather, keep flies away from her shoulder graze and any spots where scabs have come off.\",\"Record every treatment (date, product, amount and how she responded) and keep a note of exactly where she was found.\"],\"daily_checks\":[\"Eating and drinking\",\"Droppings normal\",\"Shoulder graze clean\",\"Left eye clear\",\"No flies or maggots\"],\"log_days\":14,\"feed_calc\":{\"applies\":false,\"what\":\"\",\"weight_g\":24300,\"stage\":\"Adult\",\"percent_body_weight_per_day\":null,\"feeds_per_day\":null,\"basis\":\"Adult wombat eating grass and hay; she is not fed a measured amount.\",\"sure\":true},\"release\":{\"ready_when\":[\"All mange scabs have gone and her skin has healed\",\"Shoulder graze and left eye have fully healed, and she needs no more medical care\",\"She sees and moves normally, eats well and holds her weight\",\"She is wary of people and behaves like a wild wombat\"],\"target_weight\":\"\",\"how\":[\"Release her within 24 hours of her being ready.\",\"Release about an hour after dusk, never in extreme heat, cold or storms.\",\"Ideally put her at the entrance of an unoccupied burrow within about 150 m of where she was found.\",\"Open the box and let her leave in her own time.\"],\"where\":\"Release her where she was found near Kilcunda, or if that spot is unsafe, at the closest suitable place within her home range without crossing a barrier she would not normally cross, such as the highway.\"},\"escalate\":{\"vet\":[\"She stops eating for more than a day or loses weight between weekly weigh-ins.\",\"Her shoulder graze becomes red, swollen, smelly or oozing.\",\"Her left eye gets more cracked, cloudy, weepy or starts to close.\",\"Her scabs spread or new wounds appear.\"],\"urgent\":[\"Maggots or fly eggs in her wounds, or a rotting smell.\",\"Collapse, she cannot stand, or she has trouble breathing.\",\"Sudden weakness, very pale gums or a swollen belly after her car strike.\",\"Bleeding that won't stop.\"]},\"rehydration\":{\"needed\":false,\"weight_g\":null,\"dehydration_pct\":null,\"steps\":[]},\"species_group\":\"wombat\",\"next_visit\":\"Vet recheck in 1 week.\",\"sources\":[\"vic-rehab-guidelines-wombats\",\"vic-release-requirements\",\"vic-cop-welfare-wildlife-rehabilitation\",\"vic-rehabilitator-authorisation-conditions\",\"vic-shelter-foster-carer-authorisation\",\"vic-mange-management\",\"vic-rehab-guidelines-part-a\",\"au-sarcoptic-mange-treatment-sheet\"]}",
  "media": "{\"title\":\"Wombat named Hope returns to her burrow after nine weeks in care\",\"subtitle\":\"Media statement\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Statement\",\"text\":\"A wombat her carers named Hope has gone back to her own burrow this morning after nine weeks in care with Bunurong Wildlife Care. She was hit by a vehicle on the Bass Highway near Kilcunda in August.\\n\\nWhen Hope came into care she had severe mange over most of her body and a graze on her shoulder. Over the following weeks she had six weekly mange treatments and put on two kilograms. She went home with a full coat of fur.\\n\\nHope is one of 70 wombats Bunurong Wildlife Care has taken in so far this year. The shelter is asking drivers to slow down on the Bass Highway at dawn and dusk. It is also asking anyone who sees a wombat with mange to report it rather than trying to treat it themselves.\"},{\"heading\":\"Quote\",\"text\":\"\\\"Watching Hope walk back into her own burrow, with a full coat of fur, is exactly why we do this,\\\" said Dr Sam Lee, senior veterinarian at Bunurong Wildlife Care.\"},{\"heading\":\"What the public can do\",\"items\":[\"Slow down on the Bass Highway and other country roads at dawn and dusk.\",\"If you see a wombat with mange, report it rather than trying to treat it yourself. Note exactly where you saw it and which parts of its body are affected.\",\"Do not approach an injured adult wombat. Report it and wait for help.\",\"For sick, injured or orphaned wildlife, call Wildlife Victoria's free 24/7 emergency line on (03) 8400 7300. Have the location ready as a dropped pin or GPS coordinates.\"]},{\"heading\":\"About the organisation\",\"text\":\"Bunurong Wildlife Care is a registered wildlife shelter and charity in Inverloch, Victoria.\"},{\"heading\":\"Not for publication\",\"items\":[\"Do not publish the exact location of Hope's burrow or release site.\",\"Use only the general area (Bass Highway near Kilcunda) for where she was found.\"]}],\"due\":[],\"check\":[\"What date should the statement carry?\",\"Who is the media contact, and what are their details?\",\"Who should the public report mange wombats to?\",\"Can photos of Hope be released?\"]}",
  "witness": "{\"title\":\"Expert witness statement: Kelpie cross, 'Bluey'\",\"subtitle\":\"Case 26-1182; examined 14 October 2026\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"About me\",\"text\":\"My name is Dr Sam Lee. I hold a Bachelor of Veterinary Science with Honours (BVSc Hons) and I am a registered veterinarian in Victoria, registration number [Registration number]. I have 12 years of experience in small animal and shelter practice.\"},{\"heading\":\"Instructions\",\"text\":\"Inspector Kate Morris asked me to examine a dog seized from a property in Korumburra and to give my opinion on his health and welfare.\"},{\"heading\":\"What I relied on\",\"items\":[\"My physical examination of the dog on 14 October 2026.\",\"Blood test results from samples taken from the dog.\",\"A faecal flotation test (a microscope test of a faeces sample for parasite eggs).\",\"24 photographs taken when the dog was admitted.\",\"The dog's weight gain over the six days after my examination while he was on normal feeding.\"]},{\"heading\":\"The animal\",\"text\":\"The dog is a male Kelpie cross named Bluey. He is entire (not desexed). He is black and tan. I estimated his age at about four years from his teeth. His microchip number is 956000012345678.\"},{\"heading\":\"Examination\",\"text\":\"I examined the dog at 10.20 am on 14 October 2026 at the clinic. Inspector Kate Morris was present. The dog was bright and alert. He weighed 14.2 kg. I assessed his body condition as 2 out of 9 on the Purina body condition scale (a 9-point scale where 1 is emaciated, 4 to 5 is ideal and 9 is grossly obese). His ribs, spine and pelvic bones were easily seen, and I could not feel any fat. There was marked muscle loss over his head, spine and hindquarters. I estimated he was about 7 percent dehydrated: his gums were tacky (sticky rather than moist), and his skin tent was slow (when a fold of skin is lifted and released, it was slow to flatten). His coat was dull, with a heavy flea burden and flea dirt (flea droppings, made of digested blood). Over the left hock (the ankle joint of the hind leg) there was an ulcerated wound (an open sore where the skin surface has been lost) measuring 4 cm by 3 cm. There was granulation tissue (pink, new tissue that forms as a wound heals) at the edges of the wound. The wound was matted over with hair and faeces. His nails were overgrown and curling. When offered food, he ate eagerly and rapidly. He did not vomit.\"},{\"heading\":\"Tests and treatment\",\"items\":[\"Blood tests showed mild anaemia (a low level of red blood cells). His PCV (packed cell volume, the proportion of the blood made up of red blood cells) was 30 percent.\",\"Blood tests showed low albumin (a blood protein that falls with poor nutrition or protein loss). All other blood results were normal.\",\"The faecal flotation test was positive for hookworm (an intestinal worm that feeds on blood).\",\"After my examination, the dog was given normal feeding. He gained 1.1 kg over six days.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Opinion\",\"items\":[\"1. In my opinion the dog had been underfed for at least four to six weeks, and probably longer. I base this on his marked muscle loss, his body condition score of 2 out of 9 and his weight of 14.2 kg. For a dog of his frame I would expect a weight of 20 to 22 kg.\",\"2. In my opinion the cause of his weight loss was underfeeding. My tests found no medical cause for the weight loss. He gained 1.1 kg in six days on normal feeding, which supports underfeeding as the cause. I considered the hookworm and fleas. In my opinion they would have added to his anaemia, but on their own they do not explain his weight loss.\",\"3. In my opinion the wound over the left hock was at least seven to ten days old. I base this on the granulation tissue at the edges of the wound.\",\"4. In my opinion the dog would have been in pain from the hock wound and suffering from hunger.\",\"5. In my opinion both the dog's pain from the wound and his hunger would have been obvious to anyone looking at him.\",\"6. In my opinion the dog needed veterinary attention at least a week before he was seized.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Declaration\",\"items\":[\"I understand my overriding duty is to help the court impartially on matters within my expertise, not to act as an advocate for any party.\",\"The opinions in this statement are wholly or substantially based on my specialised knowledge as a veterinarian.\",\"I have made all the inquiries I consider appropriate, and no matters of significance that I regard as relevant have, to my knowledge, been withheld.\",\"Signed: ____________________\",\"Date: ____________\"]}],\"due\":[],\"check\":[\"Veterinary registration number?\",\"Date the dog was seized?\",\"Clinic name and address?\",\"Albumin value and reference range?\",\"Any treatment besides feeding, or any limitations?\"]}"
 },
 "qld": {
  "intake": "{\"title\":\"Rescue and intake record: adult female bare-nosed wombat\",\"subtitle\":\"Wildlife Victoria case 26-04417\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Register entry\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Case number: Wildlife Victoria 26-04417\",\"Common name: Bare-nosed wombat\",\"Species code: Not recorded\",\"Date in: Not recorded\",\"Sex (M, F or U): F\",\"Age class: Adult\",\"Main injury: Graze on left shoulder; severe mange\",\"Cause: Hit by car\",\"Where found: The gravel pull-over on the south side of the highway, just east of the Kilcunda bridge (Bass Highway near Kilcunda)\",\"Status: In care\"]},{\"heading\":\"Rescue details\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Hit by a car last night on the Bass Highway near Kilcunda.\",\"A motorist called it in at about 9 pm.\",\"The carer picked her up at 9.40 pm.\",\"Wildlife Victoria case 26-04417.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Where it was found\",\"text\":\"The gravel pull-over on the south side of the highway, just east of the Kilcunda bridge (Bass Highway near Kilcunda).\",\"items\":[]},{\"heading\":\"Animal\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Species: Bare-nosed wombat\",\"Sex: Female\",\"Age: Adult\",\"Weight: About 24 kg\"]},{\"heading\":\"Condition on arrival\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Pouch empty\",\"Severe mange over both flanks and back\",\"Crusting on both ears\",\"Graze on the left shoulder\",\"Walking okay, no obvious fractures\",\"Alert and hissing\"]},{\"heading\":\"First aid and care given\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Placed in the quiet pen with a hide box and water.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Vet\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Booked with the Wildlife Victoria travelling vet for tomorrow morning.\"]}],\"due\":[{\"what\":\"Wildlife Victoria travelling vet visit\",\"when\":\"Tomorrow morning\"}],\"check\":[\"What was the rescue date?\",\"Is there an RSPCA Queensland case number?\",\"This is a Victorian case. Which register applies?\"]}",
  "log": "{\"title\":\"Oliver (eastern grey kangaroo joey) care log\",\"subtitle\":\"Thursday\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Summary\",\"text\":\"Oliver gained weight, took all four bottle feeds and is spending more time out of the pouch, with a small tail scrape cleaned today.\"},{\"heading\":\"Weight\",\"text\":\"2.31 kg this morning, up 40 g from Monday.\"},{\"heading\":\"Feeding\",\"items\":[\"Four bottle feeds of Wombaroo 0.7, 120 mL each.\",\"Took all feeds; the last one a bit slowly.\",\"Started picking at kangaroo grass.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Toileting\",\"text\":\"Toileted after every feed with stimulation. Normal pellets.\"},{\"heading\":\"Behaviour and condition\",\"text\":\"Spending more time out of the pouch and hopping around the room.\"},{\"heading\":\"Wounds and treatment\",\"text\":\"Small scrape on the tail from the pen door, cleaned with saline.\"},{\"heading\":\"To watch\",\"items\":[\"Tail scrape from the pen door. Consider a vet check if it doesn't heal well.\",\"Last feed taken a bit slowly.\"]}],\"due\":[{\"what\":\"Next weigh-in\",\"when\":\"Monday\"}],\"check\":[\"Was the pen door fixed after the scrape?\"]}",
  "carer-carer": "{\"animal\":\"Common ringtail possum joey (female)\",\"species\":\"Common ringtail possum (Pseudocheirus peregrinus)\",\"kind\":\"wildlife\",\"summary\":\"This furred, eyes-open female joey of about 120 g came into care yesterday after a cat attack. The vet checked her this morning, found no puncture wounds and dispensed an antibiotic. The goal is to keep her warm and well fed, wean her onto native browse alongside other ringtails, and soft-release her near where she was found.\",\"photo_findings\":[],\"medications\":[{\"name\":\"Antibiotic dispensed by the clinic (to prevent infection after the cat attack)\",\"how\":\"By mouth, at the amount dispensed by the clinic\",\"how_often\":\"Once a day, at the same time each day\",\"how_long\":\"5 days, finishing the full course\",\"tip\":\"Give it slowly from a small syringe at the side of her mouth so she can swallow comfortably.\"}],\"plan\":[{\"when\":\"Right now and the next few hours\",\"tasks\":[\"Settle her in her pouch on the warmed heat pad, cover the enclosure and leave her in a quiet, dim room to rest.\",\"Offer her next milk feed only once she feels warm to the touch and is alert.\"]},{\"when\":\"Day 5 of the antibiotic\",\"tasks\":[\"Give the last antibiotic dose.\"]},{\"when\":\"Coming weeks, as she eats more leaves\",\"tasks\":[\"Gradually increase the browse as she eats more, and move her to a larger enclosure with branches once she is spending most of her time out of the pouch.\",\"Arrange through your group for her to join other young ringtails with an experienced carer, so she grows up and is released with her own kind.\"]},{\"when\":\"Weaning (about 160 to 180 days of age)\",\"tasks\":[\"Slowly reduce her milk until she is living fully on native browse, which usually happens at about 310 g.\"]}],\"housing\":[\"Keep her in a soft pouch inside a small, secure enclosure, on a heat pad set so the pouch stays at about 30°C, as the vet advised.\",\"Leave part of the enclosure off the heat pad so she can move away if she gets too warm.\",\"Cover the enclosure and keep it in a quiet room, well away from pets, children and noise, because stress is dangerous for joeys.\",\"Remove droppings, soiled bedding and old leaves daily, and clean the food and water dishes daily with a non-toxic disinfectant.\"],\"feeding\":[\"Feed Wombaroo Possum Milk Replacer >0.8, made up as 25 g of powder (3½ scoops) with 80 mL of cool pre-boiled water to make 100 mL, then warmed to about 30°C. Give it slowly from a sterilised bottle or syringe.\",\"Feed every 4 hours through the day and evening, as the vet advised. Use the feed calculator to get the daily amount from today's weight, and sterilise the bottles, teats and syringes before every feed.\",\"After each feed, gently stroke her genital and bottom area with a warm, damp cloth until she has a wee and a poo.\",\"Each day, offer fresh native browse such as young eucalypt leaves, plus the odd native flower, as the vet suggested. Give no fruit, vegetables or cabbage-family plants, and no lantana, oleander or hydrangea, because these cause bloat and poisoning in ringtails. Never give cow's milk, which causes diarrhoea.\",\"Offer water in a shallow dish beside her, never mixed into the milk, and give a little extra between feeds in hot weather.\"],\"activity\":[\"Handle her only for feeds, toileting and weighing. Wear gloves and wash your hands afterwards, so she stays wild and you stay safe.\",\"Add leafy branches to climb on and hide in as she grows more active.\",\"It is normal for her to eat some of her own droppings, because she needs them for healthy digestion.\"],\"care\":[\"Weigh her every day at the same time, before the first feed, and record it so the feed amount stays right.\",\"Keep a register of the exact rescue location, every medication dose, daily weights, feeds and the outcome.\"],\"daily_checks\":[\"Weighed before feeding\",\"Antibiotic given\",\"Wees and poos normal\",\"Fresh browse offered\",\"Pouch warm, not hot\"],\"log_days\":7,\"feed_calc\":{\"applies\":true,\"what\":\"Wombaroo Possum Milk Replacer >0.8\",\"weight_g\":120,\"stage\":\"Furred, eyes open, about 100 to 140 days, more than 80% of pouch life\",\"percent_body_weight_per_day\":14,\"feeds_per_day\":4,\"basis\":\"Wombaroo Milk Booklet Common Ringtail chart: about 17 mL/day at 120 g (between 12 mL at 62 g and 18 mL at 135 g); the product page gives 2 to 4 feeds a day.\",\"sure\":false},\"release\":{\"ready_when\":[\"Fully weaned and finding and eating native browse herself.\",\"Around 600 g, with any injuries healed and in good health.\",\"Wary of people and pets, not humanised.\",\"Assessed as physically and behaviourally fit by a wildlife vet or an experienced rehabilitator.\"],\"target_weight\":\"About 600 g, as the vet advised\",\"how\":[\"Release her together with the ringtails she was raised with.\",\"Use a soft release: put a nest box or drey at the site, and provide fresh browse and water there while she settles.\",\"Release at dusk, when ringtails become active, and never in storms, heat or cold snaps.\"],\"where\":\"Release her at the exact rescue site near Wonthaggi if it has suitable trees, shelter, water and food and is free of hazards; otherwise release as close to it as possible.\"},\"escalate\":{\"vet\":[\"Any swelling, redness, discharge, limping or a wound appearing, as cat injuries can show up late.\",\"Diarrhoea, or droppings that change colour or become runny.\",\"Refusing feeds, or no weight gain over two days in a row.\",\"Seeming quieter, weaker or less interested than usual.\"],\"urgent\":[\"Cold, floppy, collapsed or unable to swallow. Give nothing by mouth, keep her warm and go straight to a vet.\",\"Difficult, noisy or open-mouth breathing.\",\"A swollen, tight belly (bloat).\",\"Any bleeding.\"]},\"rehydration\":{\"needed\":false,\"weight_g\":null,\"dehydration_pct\":null,\"steps\":[]},\"species_group\":\"ringtail\",\"next_visit\":\"\",\"sources\":[\"qld-cop-protected-animals-care\",\"qld-cop-protected-animals-release\",\"qld-gov-helping-injured-wildlife\",\"qld-des-rescue-and-rehabilitation\",\"wombaroo-milk-replacers-feeding-guide\",\"wires-possums\"]}",
  "tovet": "{\"title\":\"Vet visit summary: Willow, juvenile female wombat\",\"subtitle\":\"Carer summary for vet visit\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Text to your vet\",\"text\":\"Willow, juvenile female wombat, in care 5 weeks after her mother was hit by a car. She is on the mange treatment you prescribed, last dose 10 days ago. Her weight dropped from 6.2 to 6.0 kg over the past week. She is eating fewer pellets but still grazing, has had loose droppings for 2 days and is scratching more again. Is the weight loss a concern, does she need another mange dose, and when can she move outdoors?\",\"items\":[]},{\"heading\":\"Animal\",\"items\":[\"Species: wombat\",\"Sex: female\",\"Stage: juvenile\",\"Name: Willow\",\"Current weight: 6.0 kg\"]},{\"heading\":\"Reason for visit\",\"text\":\"Weight loss, reduced pellet intake, loose droppings and increased scratching while on mange treatment. The carer is seeking advice on further mange treatment and timing of the move to an outdoor enclosure.\",\"items\":[]},{\"heading\":\"History in care\",\"items\":[\"Came into care 5 weeks ago after her mother was hit by a vehicle on the Inverloch road.\",\"Currently on vet-prescribed mange treatment.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Recent observations\",\"items\":[\"Weight: 6.2 kg to 6.0 kg over the past week.\",\"Feeding: eating fewer pellets, still grazing.\",\"Toileting: loose droppings for 2 days.\",\"Behaviour: scratching more again.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Current medications and treatment\",\"items\":[\"Mange treatment as prescribed by the vet, last dose 10 days ago.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Questions for the vet\",\"items\":[\"Is the weight drop from 6.2 kg to 6.0 kg over a week a concern?\",\"Could the loose droppings and reduced pellet intake be linked to the weight loss?\",\"Does the increased scratching mean she needs another mange dose, and if so, when?\",\"When can she move to the outdoor enclosure?\"]},{\"heading\":\"What the carer needs back\",\"items\":[\"A decision on whether to give another mange dose, with written dosing instructions if needed.\",\"Guidance on when she can move to the outdoor enclosure.\"]}],\"due\":[],\"check\":[\"What is the mange medication name and dose?\",\"How often is the mange treatment given?\",\"Is Inverloch road the correct location?\",\"What was the exact rescue date?\"]}",
  "funding": "{\"title\":\"Treating Wombat Mange in the Wild\",\"subtitle\":\"Bass Coast Shire Community Grants – Environment Stream\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Project summary\",\"text\":\"We are seeking $8,500 to buy a second wildlife ambulance trailer and a portable wombat burrow-flap kit, and to run a training day for our volunteers. The kit will let us treat mange in wombats in the wild rather than bringing every affected wombat into care. The project would run from February to December next year.\"},{\"heading\":\"About us\",\"text\":\"Bunurong Wildlife Care Inc is a registered charity and authorised wildlife shelter (Wildlife Shelter 15147751) based in Inverloch, Victoria. We rescue, rehabilitate and release native wildlife across Bass Coast and South Gippsland. Our work is carried out by 18 trained volunteers, and last year we took in 214 animals.\"},{\"heading\":\"The need\",\"text\":\"Wombats make up a large share of our work. About a third of the 214 animals we took in last year were wombats, and 60 of those wombats had mange. At present we bring every wombat with mange into care for treatment. With a burrow-flap kit, we can treat wombats where they live. A second ambulance trailer will give our volunteers more capacity on the road.\"},{\"heading\":\"What we will do\",\"items\":[\"Buy and fit out a second wildlife ambulance trailer.\",\"Buy a portable wombat burrow-flap kit and the treatment supplies to use with it.\",\"Run a training day so our volunteers can use the kit safely and effectively.\",\"Treat wombats with mange in the wild, bringing animals into care only when needed.\",\"Monitor treated wombats with trail cameras to track their recovery.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Budget\",\"items\":[\"Second wildlife ambulance trailer: $5,200\",\"Portable wombat burrow-flap kit and treatment supplies: $2,100\",\"Volunteer training day: $1,200\",\"Total requested: $8,500\"]},{\"heading\":\"Timeline\",\"text\":\"The project will run from February to December next year.\"},{\"heading\":\"Outcomes and how we will measure them\",\"items\":[\"Number of wombats treated for mange in the wild.\",\"Number of treated wombats seen recovering on trail cameras.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Who benefits\",\"items\":[\"Wombats with mange across Bass Coast and South Gippsland, which can be treated in their own burrows.\",\"Our 18 trained volunteers, through new skills and equipment.\",\"Wildlife Victoria and local vets, who refer animals to us.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Partners and support\",\"text\":\"Wildlife Victoria and local vets already refer animals to us.\"},{\"heading\":\"Why us\",\"text\":\"We are an authorised wildlife shelter with 18 trained volunteers and an established flow of referrals from Wildlife Victoria and local vets. Last year we cared for 214 animals, including 60 wombats with mange, so we know this work and the local wombat population well.\"}],\"due\":[{\"what\":\"Project start\",\"when\":\"February next year\"},{\"what\":\"Project end\",\"when\":\"December next year\"}],\"check\":[\"Which calendar year will the project run?\",\"What is the grant closing date?\",\"Do you already own trail cameras?\",\"How many wombats do you aim to treat?\",\"Can vets or Wildlife Victoria provide support letters?\"]}",
  "mop": "{\"title\":\"Short-term care plan: young common brushtail possum\",\"subtitle\":\"Caller: Jenny. Rescuer collecting tomorrow at about 8am.\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Text to send\",\"text\":\"Hi Jenny, thank you for helping the young possum. Keep it in a closed box with air holes, lined with a towel, in your dark, quiet laundry. Put the hot water bottle, wrapped in a towel, at one end of the box so it can move away if it gets too warm. Keep the dog and children away and handle it as little as possible. Food and water can wait for the rescuer. A rescuer will collect it tomorrow morning at about 8am. Please note exactly where you found it. Call back straight away if it gets cold, weak or floppy, or starts bleeding.\",\"items\":[]},{\"heading\":\"Right now\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Move it to a box with a lid, air holes and a towel lining. A shoe box is fine if it has these.\",\"Keep it warm, dark and quiet in the laundry.\",\"Handle it as little as possible.\",\"Keep the dog and children out of the laundry.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Basic first aid\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Fill the hot water bottle with warm (not hot) water. Wrap it in a towel and put it at one end of the box.\",\"Cover the box with a towel.\",\"Do not give food or water unless a vet, wildlife carer or RSPCA Queensland wildlife advises otherwise.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Getting it to a vet or shelter\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"A rescuer will collect it tomorrow morning at about 8am.\",\"This care is short-term only, until the rescuer arrives.\",\"If you need to take it anywhere, carry it in the closed box in the car cabin. Keep the car quiet, with no music, and not too hot or cold. Never hold it on your lap.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Keep yourself safe\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"Possums can scratch and bite. If you must move it, use a thick towel and gloves.\",\"Wash your hands after any contact.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Call back straight away if\",\"text\":\"\",\"items\":[\"It feels cold, cannot hold its head up, or goes weak or floppy.\",\"It breathes with difficulty or you see bleeding.\",\"The dog or a cat gets to it.\",\"RSPCA Queensland wildlife: 1300 ANIMAL (1300 264 625), 7am to 7pm. After hours, go to the nearest vet or the RSPCA Wacol Wildlife Hospital (open 24/7).\"]},{\"heading\":\"Where it was found\",\"text\":\"On the ground in her backyard in Wonthaggi, under the big gum near the back fence.\",\"items\":[]}],\"due\":[{\"what\":\"Rescuer collects the possum\",\"when\":\"Tomorrow morning, about 8am\"}],\"check\":[\"Wonthaggi is in Victoria. Is the location correct?\",\"If in Victoria, which contact number applies?\",\"Any contact with a cat?\"]}",
  "vetnotes": "{\"animals\":[{\"patient\":{\"name\":\"\",\"species\":\"Wombat\",\"breed\":\"\",\"sex\":\"Female\",\"age\":\"Adult\",\"weight_kg\":\"24.3\",\"microchip\":\"\",\"animal_id\":\"\",\"intake_type\":\"Wildlife admission: vehicle strike\"},\"visit\":{\"type\":\"Clinical consult\",\"clinician\":\"\"},\"subjective\":\"Adult female wombat struck by a vehicle on the Bass Highway near Kilcunda last night. Presented by carer this morning.\",\"objective\":{\"vitals\":{\"temp_c\":\"36.1\",\"hr_bpm\":\"82\",\"rr_bpm\":\"22\",\"bcs\":\"\",\"mm_crt\":\"MM pink, CRT <2 s\",\"hydration\":\"\"},\"findings\":[\"BAR; hissing on handling.\",\"Sarcoptic mange affecting approximately 30% of the body surface.\",\"Crusting over both pinnae.\",\"Early fissuring around the left eye.\",\"Abrasion over the left shoulder, 3 x 2 cm, superficial and contaminated.\",\"Crusting over both flanks.\",\"No fractures palpable.\",\"Ambulating normally; no neurological deficits.\",\"Pouch empty; not lactating.\"],\"photo_findings\":[]},\"assessment\":{\"problems\":[\"1. Sarcoptic mange, moderate (approximately 30% body surface; crusting of both flanks and pinnae, early periocular fissuring left eye).\",\"2. Superficial contaminated abrasion, left shoulder (3 x 2 cm).\"],\"differentials\":[],\"prognosis\":\"Good\"},\"plan\":{\"procedures\":[\"Left shoulder abrasion cleaned with dilute chlorhexidine.\"],\"diagnostics\":[],\"treatments\":[{\"drug\":\"Cydectin (moxidectin) pour-on\",\"dose\":\"0.8 mL/kg (19.4 mL), per permit\",\"route\":\"Topical\",\"frequency\":\"Once\",\"duration\":\"\",\"when\":\"given\"},{\"drug\":\"Meloxicam (Metacam)\",\"dose\":\"0.2 mg/kg\",\"route\":\"SC\",\"frequency\":\"Once\",\"duration\":\"\",\"when\":\"given\"},{\"drug\":\"Cydectin (moxidectin) pour-on\",\"dose\":\"0.8 mL/kg, per permit\",\"route\":\"Topical\",\"frequency\":\"Weekly\",\"duration\":\"15 weeks\",\"when\":\"continue\"}],\"hospital_plan\":[],\"home_care\":[\"House in a quiet pen with a hide box.\",\"Minimal handling.\",\"Carer to repeat the Cydectin pour-on weekly for 15 weeks.\",\"Weigh weekly.\"],\"follow_up\":[\"Recheck in 1 week.\"]},\"welfare_notes\":\"\",\"check\":[\"Hydration status confirmed before meloxicam?\",\"\\\"Side deck tin\\\" = Cydectin (moxidectin) pour-on?\"]}]}",
  "caresheet": "{\"animal\":\"Adult female wombat\",\"species\":\"Common (bare-nosed) wombat\",\"kind\":\"wildlife\",\"summary\":\"This adult female wombat was hit by a car and has moderate mange and a shallow graze on her left shoulder; the clinic cleaned the graze and gave her first mange treatment and a pain-relief injection. The goal is to clear her mange and heal her skin so she can go back to the wild.\",\"photo_findings\":[],\"medications\":[{\"name\":\"Cydectin pour-on (to kill the mange mites)\",\"how\":\"Poured onto her skin as shown by the clinic, at 0.8 mL per kilogram of body weight (19.4 mL at her current 24.3 kg), working the dose out from her latest weekly weight\",\"how_often\":\"Once a week, on the same day each week\",\"how_long\":\"15 weeks\",\"tip\":\"The first dose was given at the clinic, so the next one is due one week after her vet visit.\"}],\"plan\":[{\"when\":\"Week 1\",\"tasks\":[\"Give the second Cydectin dose on day 7.\",\"Take her to her recheck.\"]},{\"when\":\"Weeks 2 to 14\",\"tasks\":[\"Keep giving the weekly Cydectin doses.\"]},{\"when\":\"Week 15\",\"tasks\":[\"Give the last Cydectin dose.\"]}],\"housing\":[\"Keep her in a quiet pen with a hide box where she can get out of sight.\",\"Keep her well away from dogs, cats and other animals, and out of sight of them.\",\"Remove droppings and uneaten food every day, and clean her food and water containers daily with a non-toxic disinfectant.\"],\"feeding\":[\"Offer grasses (with roots and dirt where you can) and grass hay every day.\",\"Keep fresh water available at all times.\",\"Do not feed bread, cereal biscuits, muesli mixes or carrots, because they cause gum infections and bloat, and carrots are used as rabbit bait.\",\"Never give medicated pellets such as cattle or chicken pellets, because they can contain drugs that are fatal to wombats.\"],\"activity\":[\"Handle her as little as possible, only for weighing and her weekly treatment.\",\"She hisses when handled and is very strong, so handle her carefully and keep your hands clear of her mouth.\",\"Wear gloves when handling her or her bedding and wash your hands afterwards, as mange mites can make people itchy.\"],\"care\":[\"Weigh her once a week and write it down.\",\"Keep a record of exactly where she was found, every treatment you give, her weekly weights and her outcome.\"],\"daily_checks\":[\"Eating grass and hay\",\"Normal droppings\",\"Shoulder graze clean\",\"Left eye not worse\",\"Fresh water\"],\"log_days\":14,\"feed_calc\":{\"applies\":false,\"what\":\"\",\"weight_g\":24300,\"stage\":\"Adult\",\"percent_body_weight_per_day\":null,\"feeds_per_day\":null,\"basis\":\"Adult wombat eating grass and hay, not a measured milk feed.\",\"sure\":true},\"release\":{\"ready_when\":[\"Mange is cleared and her skin, ears and eye area have healed.\",\"Her shoulder graze has fully healed.\",\"She is eating well and holding a healthy weight.\",\"A wildlife vet or experienced rehabilitator has assessed her as fit for release.\"],\"target_weight\":\"\",\"how\":[\"Release her once her weekly treatment course is finished and she has been assessed as fit.\",\"Release her around dusk or at night, when wombats are normally active.\",\"Do not release her in weather likely to cause hardship, such as storms or extreme heat.\"],\"where\":\"Release her where she was found if it has suitable habitat, shelter, water and food away from hazards, otherwise as close to that spot as possible (but not beside the highway); she was found in Victoria, and I'm not certain how cross-border release rules apply, so check with your vet.\"},\"escalate\":{\"vet\":[\"The shoulder graze becomes red, swollen, smelly or starts oozing.\",\"Her left eye becomes more cracked, weepy or closed.\",\"She stops eating for a day or keeps losing weight.\",\"Her crusting or mange spreads despite treatment.\"],\"urgent\":[\"She collapses, is very weak or can't get up.\",\"She has trouble breathing.\",\"She has a seizure or sudden wobbliness.\",\"Any bleeding, including blood in her droppings or urine.\"]},\"rehydration\":{\"needed\":false,\"weight_g\":null,\"dehydration_pct\":null,\"steps\":[]},\"species_group\":\"wombat\",\"next_visit\":\"Recheck with the vet in 1 week.\",\"sources\":[\"qld-cop-protected-animals-care\",\"qld-cop-protected-animals-release\",\"qld-des-rescue-and-rehabilitation\",\"qld-gov-helping-injured-wildlife\",\"wires-wombats\",\"usyd-vet-emergency-signs\"]}",
  "carer-vet": "{\"animal\":\"Adult female wombat, 24.3 kg\",\"species\":\"Common (bare-nosed) wombat\",\"kind\":\"wildlife\",\"summary\":\"Thank you for caring for this wombat. She is an adult female with moderate mange and a shallow graze on her left shoulder; she had her first Cydectin (moxidectin) mange treatment and a pain-relief injection of meloxicam (Metacam) at the clinic today, and the goal is to clear the mange and heal her skin so she can go home.\",\"photo_findings\":[],\"medications\":[{\"name\":\"Cydectin pour-on (moxidectin), for mange\",\"how\":\"Pour 19.4 mL along her spine, wearing gloves and eye protection\",\"how_often\":\"Once a week, on the same day each week\",\"how_long\":\"15 weeks in total (the first dose was given today, so 14 more doses)\",\"tip\":\"Cydectin is a registered poison, so keep it away from drains, dams and creeks, as it is toxic to water insects.\"}],\"plan\":[{\"when\":\"In 1 week\",\"tasks\":[\"Give the second weekly Cydectin dose.\"]},{\"when\":\"Week 15\",\"tasks\":[\"Give the last Cydectin dose, then have her assessed for release.\"]}],\"housing\":[\"Keep her in a quiet pen with a hide box and straw bedding, well away from dogs.\",\"Keep her apart from all other animals, as mange spreads by contact and mites can survive in bedding for up to 3 weeks.\",\"Hot-wash and hot-dry bedding and cloths, and remove droppings and uneaten food daily.\",\"Keep the pen shaded and out of hot sun, as wombats overheat easily.\"],\"feeding\":[\"Offer grass hay and fresh native grasses every day.\",\"A few carrots are fine for her as an adult; keep them to a small treat.\",\"Never give bread, cereal or muesli mixes, as they cause gum infections and bloat.\",\"Never give cattle or chicken pellets, as the medicines in them can kill wombats.\",\"Keep clean water available at all times and change it daily.\"],\"activity\":[\"Handle her as little as possible, as adult wombats stress easily in care.\",\"Never put your hands near her rump or between her and a wall, as she can crush hands, bite and scratch.\",\"Wear gloves and long sleeves around her, as mange can give people an itchy rash.\"],\"care\":[\"Clean the shoulder graze once a day with saline, wearing gloves.\",\"Weigh her once a week on the same scales and write down her weight, treatments and any changes.\"],\"daily_checks\":[\"Eating hay and grass\",\"Drinking water\",\"Graze clean and dry\",\"Skin round eye\",\"Bright and steady\"],\"log_days\":7,\"feed_calc\":{\"applies\":false,\"what\":\"\",\"weight_g\":24300,\"stage\":\"Adult\",\"percent_body_weight_per_day\":null,\"feeds_per_day\":null,\"basis\":\"Adult wombat eating hay and grasses, not milk-fed\",\"sure\":true},\"release\":{\"ready_when\":[\"Coat is growing back over the mange areas.\",\"Skin, including the shoulder graze, has healed.\",\"She is eating well and holding her weight.\",\"A vet or experienced rehabilitator has assessed her as fit for release.\"],\"target_weight\":\"About her admission weight of 24 kg or more\",\"how\":[\"Carry her in a ventilated crate inside the car, never in the boot.\",\"Release at dusk, when wombats are naturally active.\",\"Avoid hot, stormy or very wet weather.\",\"Open the crate near cover or burrows and let her walk out in her own time.\"],\"where\":\"Release her back where she was found near the Kilcunda pull-over, in suitable habitat away from the road edge.\"},\"escalate\":{\"vet\":[\"She stops eating.\",\"The graze becomes hot, swollen or smelly.\",\"The skin around her eye bleeds.\",\"Crusting or hair loss spreads, or she loses weight.\"],\"urgent\":[\"Wobbliness, drowsiness, drooling or lying down after a Cydectin dose.\",\"Heat stress: red feet, drooling onto her forelegs and chest, or lying on her back.\",\"Laboured breathing or collapse.\",\"Maggots in the graze or skin.\"]},\"rehydration\":{\"needed\":false,\"weight_g\":24300,\"dehydration_pct\":null,\"steps\":[]},\"species_group\":\"wombat\",\"next_visit\":\"Recheck with the vet in 1 week\",\"sources\":[\"qld-cop-protected-animals-care\",\"qld-cop-protected-animals-release\",\"qld-des-rescue-and-rehabilitation\",\"wires-wombats\"]}",
  "witness": "{\"title\":\"Expert witness statement: Kelpie cross, 'Bluey'\",\"subtitle\":\"Case 26-1182; examined 14 October 2026\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"About me\",\"text\":\"My name is Dr Sam Lee. I hold a Bachelor of Veterinary Science with Honours (BVSc Hons) and I am a registered veterinarian in Victoria, registration number [Registration number]. I have 12 years of experience in small animal and shelter practice.\"},{\"heading\":\"Instructions\",\"text\":\"Inspector Kate Morris asked me to examine a dog seized from a property in Korumburra and to give my opinion on his health and welfare.\"},{\"heading\":\"What I relied on\",\"items\":[\"My physical examination of the dog on 14 October 2026.\",\"Blood test results from samples taken from the dog.\",\"A faecal flotation test (a microscope test of a faeces sample for parasite eggs).\",\"24 photographs taken when the dog was admitted.\",\"The dog's weight gain over the six days after my examination while he was on normal feeding.\"]},{\"heading\":\"The animal\",\"text\":\"The dog is a male Kelpie cross named Bluey. He is entire (not desexed). He is black and tan. I estimated his age at about four years from his teeth. His microchip number is 956000012345678.\"},{\"heading\":\"Examination\",\"text\":\"I examined the dog at 10.20 am on 14 October 2026 at the clinic. Inspector Kate Morris was present. The dog was bright and alert. He weighed 14.2 kg. I assessed his body condition as 2 out of 9 on the Purina body condition scale (a 9-point scale where 1 is emaciated, 4 to 5 is ideal and 9 is grossly obese). His ribs, spine and pelvic bones were easily seen, and I could not feel any fat. There was marked muscle loss over his head, spine and hindquarters. I estimated he was about 7 percent dehydrated: his gums were tacky (sticky rather than moist), and his skin tent was slow (when a fold of skin is lifted and released, it was slow to flatten). His coat was dull, with a heavy flea burden and flea dirt (flea droppings, made of digested blood). Over the left hock (the ankle joint of the hind leg) there was an ulcerated wound (an open sore where the skin surface has been lost) measuring 4 cm by 3 cm. There was granulation tissue (pink, new tissue that forms as a wound heals) at the edges of the wound. The wound was matted over with hair and faeces. His nails were overgrown and curling. When offered food, he ate eagerly and rapidly. He did not vomit.\"},{\"heading\":\"Tests and treatment\",\"items\":[\"Blood tests showed mild anaemia (a low level of red blood cells). His PCV (packed cell volume, the proportion of the blood made up of red blood cells) was 30 percent.\",\"Blood tests showed low albumin (a blood protein that falls with poor nutrition or protein loss). All other blood results were normal.\",\"The faecal flotation test was positive for hookworm (an intestinal worm that feeds on blood).\",\"After my examination, the dog was given normal feeding. He gained 1.1 kg over six days.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Opinion\",\"items\":[\"1. In my opinion the dog had been underfed for at least four to six weeks, and probably longer. I base this on his marked muscle loss, his body condition score of 2 out of 9 and his weight of 14.2 kg. For a dog of his frame I would expect a weight of 20 to 22 kg.\",\"2. In my opinion the cause of his weight loss was underfeeding. My tests found no medical cause for the weight loss. He gained 1.1 kg in six days on normal feeding, which supports underfeeding as the cause. I considered the hookworm and fleas. In my opinion they would have added to his anaemia, but on their own they do not explain his weight loss.\",\"3. In my opinion the wound over the left hock was at least seven to ten days old. I base this on the granulation tissue at the edges of the wound.\",\"4. In my opinion the dog would have been in pain from the hock wound and suffering from hunger.\",\"5. In my opinion both the dog's pain from the wound and his hunger would have been obvious to anyone looking at him.\",\"6. In my opinion the dog needed veterinary attention at least a week before he was seized.\"]},{\"heading\":\"Declaration\",\"items\":[\"I understand my overriding duty is to help the court impartially on matters within my expertise, not to act as an advocate for any party.\",\"The opinions in this statement are wholly or substantially based on my specialised knowledge as a veterinarian.\",\"I have made all the inquiries I consider appropriate, and no matters of significance that I regard as relevant have, to my knowledge, been withheld.\",\"Signed: ____________________\",\"Date: ____________\"]}],\"due\":[],\"check\":[\"Veterinary registration number?\",\"Date the dog was seized?\",\"Clinic name and address?\",\"Albumin value and reference range?\",\"Any treatment besides feeding, or any limitations?\"]}",
  "media": "{\"title\":\"Wombat Hope returns home after nine weeks in care\",\"subtitle\":\"Media statement, Inverloch\",\"sections\":[{\"heading\":\"Statement\",\"text\":\"A wombat named Hope went back to her own burrow this morning after nine weeks in the care of Bunurong Wildlife Care, following her rescue after she was hit by a vehicle on the Bass Highway near Kilcunda in August.\\n\\nWhen Hope came into care she had severe mange over most of her body and a graze on her shoulder. Mange is a skin disease that causes hair loss. Hope was given six weekly mange treatments and put on two kilograms during her recovery. Her carers, who gave her the name Hope, saw her walk back into her burrow with a full coat of fur.\\n\\nHope is one of 70 wombats Bunurong Wildlife Care has taken in so far this year. We are asking drivers to slow down on the Bass Highway at dawn and dusk, when wombats are most likely to be on the move. We also ask anyone who sees a wombat with mange to report it rather than try to treat it themselves, so it can get the right care.\"},{\"heading\":\"Quote\",\"text\":\"\\\"Watching her walk back into her own burrow, with a full coat of fur, is exactly why we do this,\\\" said Dr Sam Lee, senior veterinarian at Bunurong Wildlife Care.\"},{\"heading\":\"What the public can do\",\"items\":[\"Slow down on the Bass Highway at dawn and dusk, when wombats are most active.\",\"If you see a wombat with mange, report it to a local wildlife rescue group or vet rather than trying to treat it yourself.\",\"When reporting, note exactly where you saw the wombat and which parts of its body look affected.\",\"Do not approach an injured adult wombat. Keep your distance, report it and wait for help.\"]},{\"heading\":\"About the organisation\",\"text\":\"Bunurong Wildlife Care is a registered wildlife shelter and charity in Inverloch, Victoria. It has taken in 70 wombats so far this year.\"},{\"heading\":\"Not for publication\",\"items\":[\"Do not publish the exact location of Hope's burrow.\",\"Release photos should not show landmarks that could identify the burrow site.\"]}],\"due\":[],\"check\":[\"What date should the release carry?\",\"Who is the media contact, with phone and email?\",\"Include a Victorian wildlife emergency number?\",\"Are photos of Hope available for release?\"]}"
 }
};
